WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.632 --> 00:00:05.085
(heart thumping music)

00:00:05.085 --> 00:00:06.401
- Three, two--

00:00:06.401 --> 00:00:07.324
- One!

00:00:07.324 --> 00:00:08.590
(cheering)

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.257
- [Woman] Coming up.

00:00:16.262 --> 00:00:17.765
(adventurous music)

00:00:17.765 --> 00:00:20.277
- [Narrator] Man kind and the elements.

00:00:20.277 --> 00:00:23.748
For some, it's an uncomfortable bond.

00:00:23.748 --> 00:00:25.196
- [Man] It's getting worse out here guys!

00:00:25.196 --> 00:00:26.187
- [Narrator] For others,

00:00:26.187 --> 00:00:30.025
when weather strikes, inspiration begins.

00:00:30.025 --> 00:00:32.225
- [Man] You come to a place like this

00:00:32.225 --> 00:00:35.596
the stars just burst.

00:00:35.596 --> 00:00:36.429
- [Narrator] These are the people

00:00:36.429 --> 00:00:38.567
who challenge nature,

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:40.487
seek out its limits,

00:00:40.487 --> 00:00:43.957
reveal its secrets and
embrace its awesome power.

00:00:43.957 --> 00:00:45.707
- Woo hoo!

00:00:47.786 --> 00:00:49.471
- [Narrator] In this episode we'll meet

00:00:49.471 --> 00:00:52.346
a group of civilians
putting their lives at risk

00:00:52.346 --> 00:00:55.977
to rescue people trapped in
Iceland's extreme environments.

00:00:55.977 --> 00:00:57.097
- [Man] When you get cooled down

00:00:57.097 --> 00:01:00.153
your heart goes pumping
almost out of your chest.

00:01:00.153 --> 00:01:01.892
- [Narrator] A man who
climbs hundreds of feet

00:01:01.892 --> 00:01:03.953
in the air in the hopes of protecting

00:01:03.953 --> 00:01:06.964
some of the oldest trees on earth.

00:01:06.964 --> 00:01:08.910
And a team of sailors racing up

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.851
the treacherous coastline
of the Pacific Northwest.

00:01:11.851 --> 00:01:13.007
(radio chatter)

00:01:13.007 --> 00:01:15.132
- Yeah, we're really scared.

00:01:15.132 --> 00:01:17.460
- [Narrator] These pioneers
of the great outdoors ahead,

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.043
on That's Amazing.

00:01:25.853 --> 00:01:29.270
(bouncy energetic music)

00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:34.634
- The moment right before I jump

00:01:34.634 --> 00:01:36.301
(slow breathing)
there's a split second there

00:01:36.301 --> 00:01:39.509
where everything goes completely silent.

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:41.429
And that's when I push off the edge.

00:01:41.429 --> 00:01:44.429
Three, two, one, see ya!

00:01:46.181 --> 00:01:48.320
(adventurous music)

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.653
Woo hoo!

00:01:50.561 --> 00:01:52.931
- [Narrator] We have all wanted to fly,

00:01:52.931 --> 00:01:55.910
but who would willingly throw
themselves off a mountain?

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:57.078
- [Ellen Voiceover] My
name is Ellen Brennan,

00:01:57.078 --> 00:01:59.515
and I'm a wingsuit base jumper.

00:01:59.515 --> 00:02:02.214
Currently I'm the fastest
flying woman in the world

00:02:02.214 --> 00:02:05.434
when it comes to wingsuit base jumping.

00:02:05.434 --> 00:02:06.725
- [Narrator] A wingsuit is a special suit

00:02:06.725 --> 00:02:07.954
that inflates with air

00:02:07.954 --> 00:02:12.037
and allows you to, in
essence, fly or glide.

00:02:12.988 --> 00:02:16.824
Ellen does this all over
the world at crazy speeds.

00:02:16.824 --> 00:02:17.836
- [Ellen] On average, I'm flying

00:02:17.836 --> 00:02:21.336
about 180, 190 kilometers per hour.

00:02:21.336 --> 00:02:24.125
- [Narrator] That's
almost 120 miles an hour.

00:02:24.125 --> 00:02:25.714
- [Ellen] So when I'm
going to go do a jump,

00:02:25.714 --> 00:02:28.693
I'm focusing on what's
happening weather wise.

00:02:28.693 --> 00:02:30.519
I'm looking at the trees to
see if there's wind blowing,

00:02:30.519 --> 00:02:33.063
I'm watching the sun to see
if it's heating up the air.

00:02:33.063 --> 00:02:35.520
I don't want to push my
luck with the mountain.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:36.820
My vision is the first thing I notice

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.404
so everything becomes very focused

00:02:38.404 --> 00:02:39.801
on just my feet.

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:42.319
(slow cracking sound)

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:44.140
(metronome ticking)
my heart's beating

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:45.304
super fast, usually beating

00:02:45.304 --> 00:02:47.887
like 160, 170 beats per minute.

00:02:48.915 --> 00:02:52.081
Sometimes people will talk to
me and I won't even hear them.

00:02:52.081 --> 00:02:53.257
And as soon as I push off I can hear

00:02:53.257 --> 00:02:56.628
the splits of my feet off the rocks.

00:02:56.628 --> 00:02:59.711
(wind breezing past)

00:03:01.188 --> 00:03:02.472
(adventurous music)

00:03:02.472 --> 00:03:03.722
Woo hoo!

00:03:05.119 --> 00:03:06.937
Like it's something that shouldn't happen.

00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:10.613
It's not natural, but I'm doing it.

00:03:10.613 --> 00:03:13.410
How in the world did I
get to be able to do that?

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.787
It’s one of the coolest things in the world.

00:03:14.787 --> 00:03:16.535
Like, I can't think of anything better to do.

00:03:16.535 --> 00:03:18.305
(chuckle)

00:03:18.305 --> 00:03:20.388
It's pretty mind-blowing.

00:03:22.017 --> 00:03:25.023
(frantic music)

00:03:25.023 --> 00:03:27.927
- [Narrator] In the dangerous
sees of the Pacific Northwest,

00:03:27.927 --> 00:03:30.377
44 sailboats are competing
in one of the longest

00:03:30.377 --> 00:03:33.984
and most grueling human
powered races on Earth.

00:03:33.984 --> 00:03:37.033
The race to Alaska is a 750 mile journey

00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:41.188
and the only way forward
is by wind or by oar.

00:03:41.188 --> 00:03:43.560
Here the water and weather conditions

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.699
are extremely unpredictable.

00:03:45.699 --> 00:03:49.760
If you get stuck in
them, you're on your own.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.038
- [Man] It's getting worse out here guys!

00:03:51.038 --> 00:03:53.206
We have to go to Bella Bella.

00:03:53.206 --> 00:03:54.726
- I don't think there's any other sport

00:03:54.726 --> 00:03:57.169
that relies so heavily

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:59.439
on the weather to propel you.

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:00.959
But you also have to respect it,

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:04.057
otherwise it will destroy you.

00:04:04.057 --> 00:04:06.394
- [Narrator] Spencer Weber
is with team Hot Mess.

00:04:06.394 --> 00:04:08.319
A crew of four young Canadians

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:10.555
with little endurance sailing experience.

00:04:10.555 --> 00:04:12.313
They're racing against faster boats

00:04:12.313 --> 00:04:14.044
with more seasoned crews

00:04:14.044 --> 00:04:15.815
in a sail contest that knocks out

00:04:15.815 --> 00:04:19.133
more than half the teams that attempt it.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:21.334
So for these intrepid young sailors,

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:22.767
it's not about winning.

00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:26.934
It's about making it to the
finish line in one piece.

00:04:30.775 --> 00:04:33.956
- Race to Alaska is a 750 mile engineless,

00:04:33.956 --> 00:04:35.372
unsupported boat race

00:04:35.372 --> 00:04:38.209
from Port Townsend, Washington
to Ketchikan, Alaska.

00:04:38.209 --> 00:04:40.119
- And there's only two
checkpoints in between.

00:04:40.119 --> 00:04:42.569
You have to go through Seymour Narrows

00:04:42.569 --> 00:04:44.489
and you have to go through Bella Bella.

00:04:44.489 --> 00:04:46.836
But other than that, it's
choose your own adventure.

00:04:46.836 --> 00:04:48.067
- [Jake] The race is really unconventional

00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:50.387
because sailing has so many rules.

00:04:50.387 --> 00:04:52.086
So we want to strip it all away

00:04:52.086 --> 00:04:53.907
and make it just about any boat

00:04:53.907 --> 00:04:55.584
no handicaps or anything,

00:04:55.584 --> 00:04:57.262
and if you can make it first

00:04:57.262 --> 00:04:59.262
you get $10,000.

00:04:59.262 --> 00:05:00.640
- There's a lot of different battles

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.131
going on in the R2AK.

00:05:02.131 --> 00:05:04.310
Fellow racers who you're
trying to compete with,

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.920
but you're also trying to overcome

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.371
these enormous forces of
nature, wind and current.

00:05:10.371 --> 00:05:11.967
And you're just in this tiny little boat

00:05:11.967 --> 00:05:14.844
trying to take on these
challenges head on.

00:05:14.844 --> 00:05:16.928
- [Narrator] Team Hot Mess
are well aware of the dangers

00:05:16.928 --> 00:05:19.918
inherent with sailing in
the Pacific Northwest.

00:05:19.918 --> 00:05:20.751
They train for the race

00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:22.729
in nearby Vancouver, British Columbia.

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:24.972
- So we had four crew.

00:05:24.972 --> 00:05:26.870
It was myself, Neil Roberts,

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:28.721
Will Schwenger, and Nick Schwenger.

00:05:28.721 --> 00:05:31.161
We met here and decided
to do the race together.

00:05:31.161 --> 00:05:32.994
I was a civil engineer

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.364
in the middle of a quarter-life crisis,

00:05:36.364 --> 00:05:39.065
and it was between
staying in my cozy cubicle

00:05:39.065 --> 00:05:42.373
or going on a suffer fest to Alaska.

00:05:42.373 --> 00:05:45.004
So, decided to go to Alaska.

00:05:45.004 --> 00:05:45.961
Anyone who tells you they're

00:05:45.961 --> 00:05:48.398
doing it for the 10 grand is lying.

00:05:48.398 --> 00:05:50.027
Because the guys who are going to win it

00:05:50.027 --> 00:05:52.656
are doing it in
hydrofoiling, carbon fiber,

00:05:52.656 --> 00:05:56.855
fancy boats and to them 10
grand is just chump change.

00:05:56.855 --> 00:05:58.776
For me, I just love sailing

00:05:58.776 --> 00:06:00.353
and the R2AK seemed like

00:06:00.353 --> 00:06:03.270
the steepest learning curve around.

00:06:04.784 --> 00:06:09.425
- [Crowd] Five, four, three, two, one!

00:06:09.425 --> 00:06:10.258
(cheering)

00:06:10.258 --> 00:06:11.118
- [Spencer] The race started that noon.

00:06:11.118 --> 00:06:13.367
Everyone sprinting down
the docks to their boat,

00:06:13.367 --> 00:06:15.469
jumping on the boat, untying it,

00:06:15.469 --> 00:06:17.896
and then everyone's using
their human propulsion method

00:06:17.896 --> 00:06:19.246
to get out of the harbor.

00:06:19.246 --> 00:06:22.519
So everyone's rowing and
pedaling and paddling

00:06:22.519 --> 00:06:25.058
as quickly as they can to get out.

00:06:25.058 --> 00:06:27.349
The adrenaline's going and
everyone's super stoked

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:29.279
and the atmosphere is just wild.

00:06:29.279 --> 00:06:30.981
- [Man] Sails are up around the corner.

00:06:30.981 --> 00:06:33.458
- [Man] We're finally
reaching the left, boys.

00:06:33.458 --> 00:06:35.797
- [Spencer] Rounded the point
out of Victoria Harbor there.

00:06:35.797 --> 00:06:37.637
Was awesome wind.

00:06:37.637 --> 00:06:39.629
- [Man] All right, first transition zone.

00:06:39.629 --> 00:06:41.796
Oh yeah, we're sailing out.

00:06:42.949 --> 00:06:46.146
(seagulls squawking)

00:06:46.146 --> 00:06:49.549
(intense music)

00:06:49.549 --> 00:06:51.227
- When you get further north,

00:06:51.227 --> 00:06:54.531
you have a great deal of current.

00:06:54.531 --> 00:06:57.088
That wind against tide condition

00:06:57.088 --> 00:06:58.851
can cause standing waves.

00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:01.381
It will be very difficult
for these vessels

00:07:01.381 --> 00:07:04.701
to try and get through
that area of the coast.

00:07:04.701 --> 00:07:05.989
- [Spencer] As soon as
we rounded Chatham Point

00:07:05.989 --> 00:07:08.669
and entered Johnstone
Strait, the gales picked up.

00:07:08.669 --> 00:07:10.659
We had like 35 knots on the nose

00:07:10.659 --> 00:07:13.651
and we're like bashing through
all these standing waves.

00:07:13.651 --> 00:07:15.309
And we have a pretty light boat

00:07:15.309 --> 00:07:17.488
so we were some hang time off waves.

00:07:17.488 --> 00:07:20.821
It was making all kinds of crazy sounds.

00:07:23.231 --> 00:07:25.549
And then once you get
through the Johnstone Strait,

00:07:25.549 --> 00:07:29.046
you're going up the outside of BC.

00:07:29.046 --> 00:07:29.879
Then you're in open ocean

00:07:29.879 --> 00:07:31.816
and that presents its own challenges.

00:07:31.816 --> 00:07:33.984
Lots of swells and no shelter from wind.

00:07:33.984 --> 00:07:36.344
And you feel so alone.

00:07:36.344 --> 00:07:37.864
It's really humbling,

00:07:37.864 --> 00:07:41.242
especially when a storm
starts ripping through.

00:07:41.242 --> 00:07:43.405
- [Woman over radio]
Gale warning in effect.

00:07:43.405 --> 00:07:46.507
Wind now 1.25 knots

00:07:46.507 --> 00:07:48.264
increasing to south two five

00:07:48.264 --> 00:07:49.452
- [Man] That's our net expense.

00:07:49.452 --> 00:07:50.285
- [Man] Good.

00:07:50.285 --> 00:07:53.602
- [Spencer] The forecast
was for 35 to 40 knots

00:07:53.602 --> 00:07:54.665
(radio chatter in background)
and we were seeing

00:07:54.665 --> 00:07:58.134
like maybe five to like seven knots.

00:07:58.134 --> 00:07:59.845
We didn't listen to the weather forecast

00:07:59.845 --> 00:08:02.925
and just got slammed by 35 knots of wind.

00:08:02.925 --> 00:08:04.864
- [Man] It's getting worse out here, guys!

00:08:04.864 --> 00:08:06.912
- Yeah, we're really scared right now

00:08:06.912 --> 00:08:08.592
so we turned around.

00:08:08.592 --> 00:08:09.584
And now we're trying

00:08:09.584 --> 00:08:12.201
to find somebody’s dock or something.

00:08:12.201 --> 00:08:15.573
But nobody lives here so
there's not a lot of docks.

00:08:15.573 --> 00:08:17.992
- [Spencer] It was
driving rain, big waves,

00:08:17.992 --> 00:08:19.323
and you couldn't see your hand

00:08:19.323 --> 00:08:21.028
in front of your face it was so dark.

00:08:21.028 --> 00:08:22.780
There were no lights on land,

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:24.518
you had no idea where anything was.

00:08:24.518 --> 00:08:26.038
We couldn't anchor because

00:08:26.038 --> 00:08:27.795
we brought insufficient anchor gear.

00:08:27.795 --> 00:08:29.442
So we just had to like keep sailing

00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:33.025
around in circles until
daylight basically.

00:08:34.748 --> 00:08:36.358
(relieving music)
(sigh)

00:08:36.358 --> 00:08:37.588
Getting caught that night before

00:08:37.588 --> 00:08:38.872
was a bit of an eye opener.

00:08:38.872 --> 00:08:39.982
from there on we kind of like

00:08:39.982 --> 00:08:42.311
took the forecasts a lot more seriously.

00:08:42.311 --> 00:08:44.220
We mapped out a bunch of bail out points

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.332
so if we'd have to stop

00:08:45.332 --> 00:08:48.121
we knew on the map like where we could go.

00:08:48.121 --> 00:08:51.183
(intense music)

00:08:51.183 --> 00:08:52.016
- [Man] Coming in to Ketchikan.

00:08:52.016 --> 00:08:52.849
- [Man] Where are they going?

00:08:52.849 --> 00:08:54.147
- Oh my god.

00:08:54.147 --> 00:08:55.531
- [Cameraman] Looking
forward to being dry?

00:08:55.531 --> 00:08:57.160
- Hell yeah.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.379
- [Spencer] You know,
we’ve been in our dry suits

00:08:58.379 --> 00:08:59.837
for 24 hours straight.

00:08:59.837 --> 00:09:01.905
Everyone's wet and cold and tired.

00:09:01.905 --> 00:09:05.488
And then the wind just
completely shut off.

00:09:06.387 --> 00:09:08.257
You're paddling into the finish line

00:09:08.257 --> 00:09:10.616
of this epic race that you've just done.

00:09:10.616 --> 00:09:11.937
It was super anticlimactic,

00:09:11.937 --> 00:09:14.007
but the race isn't about the money

00:09:14.007 --> 00:09:15.596
and it's not about the finish line;

00:09:15.596 --> 00:09:17.519
it's about the journey.

00:09:17.519 --> 00:09:20.529
There couldn't be a more poetic
end to the race to Alaska

00:09:20.529 --> 00:09:22.988
than a brutal hate mission
to the finish line.

00:09:22.988 --> 00:09:27.155
(triumphant music)
(cheering and clapping)

00:09:36.719 --> 00:09:38.879
I would definitely do the race again.

00:09:38.879 --> 00:09:39.879
You kind of need this, like,

00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:41.820
blind optimism to get through it.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.570
And the ability to forget
pain and suffering.

00:09:53.276 --> 00:09:56.072
(slow curious music)

00:09:56.072 --> 00:09:58.778
- [Narrator] On the sandy
shores of the Florida Keys,

00:09:58.778 --> 00:10:01.369
sea turtles are laying their eggs.

00:10:01.369 --> 00:10:05.456
But a threat is looming
for these gentle creatures.

00:10:05.456 --> 00:10:06.630
The Caribbean climate

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.628
has becoming increasingly unpredictable.

00:10:09.628 --> 00:10:12.530
As rising sea levels cripple the beaches

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:15.970
where these turtles annually nest.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:18.258
- They come back to their natal beaches,

00:10:18.258 --> 00:10:19.738
so animals that were born here

00:10:19.738 --> 00:10:22.184
are coming back to this
very little spit of land

00:10:22.184 --> 00:10:24.453
in order to lay their own eggs.

00:10:24.453 --> 00:10:25.493
If the beach goes away

00:10:25.493 --> 00:10:29.102
they may not be as flexible
to use another beach.

00:10:29.102 --> 00:10:30.823
(curious music)

00:10:30.823 --> 00:10:31.656
- [Narrator] Dr. Kristen Hart

00:10:31.656 --> 00:10:33.011
and her team of researchers

00:10:33.011 --> 00:10:34.485
are working around the clock

00:10:34.485 --> 00:10:36.733
tagging and tracking these turtles

00:10:36.733 --> 00:10:39.605
in an effort to protect their future.

00:10:39.605 --> 00:10:41.885
- [Kristen] They're
imperiled, they do need help,

00:10:41.885 --> 00:10:46.052
and we're trying to figure
out where the problems are.

00:10:47.165 --> 00:10:50.472
(light music)

00:10:50.472 --> 00:10:51.305
We are here on East Key

00:10:51.305 --> 00:10:53.805
in Dry Tortugas National Park.

00:10:54.891 --> 00:10:57.232
We are about 70 miles west of Key West.

00:10:57.232 --> 00:10:59.581
And we're in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:10:59.581 --> 00:11:00.730
There are seven islands,

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:02.090
we are on one of the smallest,

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:04.319
but one of the densest
turtle nesting beaches.

00:11:04.319 --> 00:11:06.010
Some of them are right
on top of each other,

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:09.031
a foot away from each other.

00:11:09.031 --> 00:11:11.708
This is a almost completely marine park

00:11:11.708 --> 00:11:15.508
and the turtles come up and
grace us with their presence.

00:11:15.508 --> 00:11:17.370
It's pretty amazing to
see these animals up close

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:18.722
and personal with them.

00:11:18.722 --> 00:11:20.889
And we get right in there.

00:11:22.273 --> 00:11:23.461
Generally sea turtles date back

00:11:23.461 --> 00:11:25.321
to the time of the dinosaurs.

00:11:25.321 --> 00:11:28.660
So it's two hundred
million years of evolution.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:29.493
Our study was the first

00:11:29.493 --> 00:11:33.099
to really concentrate on
the Dry Tortugas turtles.

00:11:33.099 --> 00:11:36.238
Our goals are to try to
determine patterns of movement

00:11:36.238 --> 00:11:40.405
and habitat use, to figure
out where they might go.

00:11:43.037 --> 00:11:46.204
(curious light music)

00:11:56.028 --> 00:11:59.944
All these points are either
sightings or capture points.

00:11:59.944 --> 00:12:01.135
11C.

00:12:01.135 --> 00:12:02.490
When we first started working here

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:06.015
we realized that we could dip
net juvenile green turtles.

00:12:06.015 --> 00:12:07.279
I had done this in the Everglades

00:12:07.279 --> 00:12:09.567
and out here the water is much more clear.

00:12:09.567 --> 00:12:14.343
We can pursue them in the
flats and shallower areas.

00:12:14.343 --> 00:12:16.414
She's coming up!

00:12:16.414 --> 00:12:17.635
The bigger turtles out here,

00:12:17.635 --> 00:12:19.502
we do what's called rodeo capture,

00:12:19.502 --> 00:12:21.419
or also turtle jumping.

00:12:22.603 --> 00:12:24.661
They're usually 10 or 15 feet down,

00:12:24.661 --> 00:12:25.902
but when they need to come get a breath

00:12:25.902 --> 00:12:27.171
they're coming up to the surface

00:12:27.171 --> 00:12:29.859
and so that's when the
diver meets them down there.

00:12:29.859 --> 00:12:32.419
You basically get your
hands sort of on the rear

00:12:32.419 --> 00:12:34.081
and right behind the head

00:12:34.081 --> 00:12:36.406
and you get the animal pointed up.

00:12:36.406 --> 00:12:37.726
I got him.

00:12:37.726 --> 00:12:39.201
It's a team effort.

00:12:39.201 --> 00:12:40.110
Once we get them onboard the boat,

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:41.379
we'll do a standard workup

00:12:41.379 --> 00:12:43.600
which involves measuring
them, weighing them, 

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:46.043
taking blood samples, tissue samples.

00:12:46.043 --> 00:12:47.571
There's a lot of questions that we can ask

00:12:47.571 --> 00:12:49.651
even just about how healthy they are,

00:12:49.651 --> 00:12:50.672
and how healthy then

00:12:50.672 --> 00:12:53.755
are the resources that they're using.

00:12:55.174 --> 00:12:57.356
(curious light music)

00:12:57.356 --> 00:13:00.144
We also fix devices on them
to track their movement.

00:13:00.144 --> 00:13:03.302
So we've used acoustic
telemetry, radio telemetry,

00:13:03.302 --> 00:13:05.152
and then satellite tracking.

00:13:05.152 --> 00:13:07.032
So we're learning a lot
about their movement paths

00:13:07.032 --> 00:13:09.449
and their areas of residence.

00:13:16.303 --> 00:13:17.492
(elegant music)

00:13:17.492 --> 00:13:18.663
They've been around the globe

00:13:18.663 --> 00:13:21.394
but they're hardwired to
come back to the very place

00:13:21.394 --> 00:13:25.135
where they were born,
to lay their own eggs.

00:13:25.135 --> 00:13:26.975
If an animal was born on East Key,

00:13:26.975 --> 00:13:28.175
she'll come back to East Key.

00:13:28.175 --> 00:13:29.792
She's not gonna go use Loggerhead Key

00:13:29.792 --> 00:13:32.292
or one of the other keys here.

00:13:37.004 --> 00:13:38.476
We all take turns every 30 minutes

00:13:38.476 --> 00:13:40.146
walking about this beach in particular

00:13:40.146 --> 00:13:42.662
takes about 10 minutes to walk around.

00:13:42.662 --> 00:13:43.904
If they nest, we let them nest

00:13:43.904 --> 00:13:46.813
and mark off where the nest
is and then we work her up,

00:13:46.813 --> 00:13:49.791
just like the same workup we
would do with a little turtle.

00:13:49.791 --> 00:13:51.052
She's corralled in a box

00:13:51.052 --> 00:13:52.340
because they can be 400 pounds

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:54.611
and they're very powerful.

00:13:54.611 --> 00:13:56.188
They'll do that about every two weeks

00:13:56.188 --> 00:13:57.257
during a season.

00:13:57.257 --> 00:13:59.620
And when we get recaptures
that's really exciting,

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:03.727
because you know the animal still
survived wherever she went.

00:14:03.727 --> 00:14:05.828
- [Narrator] In order for
turtles to lay their eggs,

00:14:05.828 --> 00:14:08.174
the conditions have to be just right.

00:14:08.174 --> 00:14:10.856
Ideally, they'll encounter high tides,

00:14:10.856 --> 00:14:14.788
dark beaches, and warm
sea surface temperatures.

00:14:14.788 --> 00:14:17.987
Otherwise, they'll do something
called a false crawl,

00:14:17.987 --> 00:14:20.489
in which a turtle will make
their way onto the beach

00:14:20.489 --> 00:14:23.089
only to turn back around.

00:14:23.089 --> 00:14:24.510
- [Kristen] We don't know
really why they false crawl

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:26.598
but something isn't right.

00:14:26.598 --> 00:14:28.140
Maybe they're not feeling contractions,

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.481
maybe the conditions just
aren't right at that spot,

00:14:30.481 --> 00:14:32.441
maybe they dig and they
hit a piece of coral

00:14:32.441 --> 00:14:35.438
or a root or something like that.

00:14:35.438 --> 00:14:37.030
(spry music)

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:39.519
- [Narrator] And false crawls
can become more frequent.

00:14:39.519 --> 00:14:40.801
Rising sea levels reduce

00:14:40.801 --> 00:14:43.350
the amount of available beach space

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:44.870
and rising temperatures

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:47.998
can drastically effect sand temperature.

00:14:47.998 --> 00:14:48.831
Studies show that

00:14:48.831 --> 00:14:51.851
cooler nest temperatures
produce more males,

00:14:51.851 --> 00:14:55.331
while warmer nests
generally produce females.

00:14:55.331 --> 00:14:56.164
- With sea level rise

00:14:56.164 --> 00:14:58.176
and climate change they're threatened.

00:14:58.176 --> 00:14:59.009
I think we're a little more concerned

00:14:59.009 --> 00:15:02.008
about their trajectory
of their population.

00:15:02.008 --> 00:15:04.593
Because it's generally
not good for the turtle.

00:15:04.593 --> 00:15:07.010
(calm music)

00:15:10.545 --> 00:15:11.378
We've learned a lot

00:15:11.378 --> 00:15:12.633
about these Dry Tortugas turtles,

00:15:12.633 --> 00:15:14.561
and it really is valuable to me

00:15:14.561 --> 00:15:15.893
to know that they value

00:15:15.893 --> 00:15:17.673
the research that we're contributing.

00:15:17.673 --> 00:15:19.065
We're answering questions
that are important

00:15:19.065 --> 00:15:21.403
that haven't been asked before.

00:15:21.403 --> 00:15:23.470
It's definitely demanding, but
at the same time, I think it's

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:26.506
one of the most rewarding
things we could ever do.

00:15:26.506 --> 00:15:28.139
There aren't many people allowed

00:15:28.139 --> 00:15:29.456
on this little patch of land

00:15:29.456 --> 00:15:32.025
so I feel very privileged
to be able to play a role

00:15:32.025 --> 00:15:33.835
in figuring these turtles out

00:15:33.835 --> 00:15:36.918
and being in this very special place.

00:15:39.121 --> 00:15:41.596
(grave music)

00:15:41.596 --> 00:15:43.654
(loud eruption)

00:15:43.654 --> 00:15:45.174
- [Narrator] Italy's Mount Etna

00:15:45.174 --> 00:15:48.523
is one of the most active
volcano's in the world.

00:15:48.523 --> 00:15:50.534
It erupts nearly every year,

00:15:50.534 --> 00:15:53.117
spewing lava and clouds of ash.

00:15:55.561 --> 00:15:57.543
But for Claudio Lanzafame,

00:15:57.543 --> 00:16:00.414
the black powder is an untapped adventure.

00:16:00.414 --> 00:16:02.240
(scraping rocks)

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:03.343
(light curious music)

00:16:03.343 --> 00:16:04.633
- My name is Claudio,

00:16:04.633 --> 00:16:07.216
and I scale ski over volcanos.

00:16:08.486 --> 00:16:10.986
(light music)

00:17:13.381 --> 00:17:16.798
(light electronic music)

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:21.473
It's incredible.

00:17:53.935 --> 00:17:57.018
(curious spry music)

00:17:57.903 --> 00:17:59.196
- [Narrator] Siggi Rafn Hilmarsson

00:17:59.196 --> 00:18:03.578
is an Icelandic baker with
an unconventional oven.

00:18:03.578 --> 00:18:06.020
He bakes bread by burying it underground

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:09.506
where it's heated for 24
hours by nearby hot springs.

00:18:09.506 --> 00:18:12.455
- It's very common to see
hot springs here in Iceland.

00:18:12.455 --> 00:18:15.756
There is constant lava crawling under us.

00:18:15.756 --> 00:18:19.138
This lava is heating up water

00:18:19.138 --> 00:18:22.407
and this water comes
boiling up on the surface.

00:18:22.407 --> 00:18:24.264
We could put it in the oven,

00:18:24.264 --> 00:18:26.431
but this is much more fun.

00:18:28.506 --> 00:18:29.676
- [Narrator] Iceland is one of

00:18:29.676 --> 00:18:31.738
the most volcanic regions in the world.

00:18:31.738 --> 00:18:35.157
With 30 active volcanos at any time.

00:18:35.157 --> 00:18:38.188
That helps create an
abundance of hot springs,

00:18:38.188 --> 00:18:40.500
some with boiling hot water.

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:43.260
And Siggi uses them to his advantage.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:45.798
He specializes in baking rúgbrauð,

00:18:45.798 --> 00:18:48.308
a traditional Icelandic rye bread recipe

00:18:48.308 --> 00:18:50.580
that dates back hundreds of years.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.900
- I know for sure that in this village

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:57.221
I can track it down as far as

00:18:57.221 --> 00:18:59.701
late 1800 something.

00:18:59.701 --> 00:19:03.479
My grandmother taught my
mother how to bake this bread

00:19:03.479 --> 00:19:06.066
and my mother taught me.

00:19:06.066 --> 00:19:08.959
In the hot spring rye bread recipe

00:19:08.959 --> 00:19:11.266
we have rye, flower, sugar,

00:19:11.266 --> 00:19:14.116
baking powder, salt, and milk.

00:19:14.116 --> 00:19:15.311
We put this in a pot,

00:19:15.311 --> 00:19:17.334
we put butter in the pot

00:19:17.334 --> 00:19:19.746
then we wrap it with plastic foil

00:19:19.746 --> 00:19:21.278
and we put it down

00:19:21.278 --> 00:19:24.278
in our hot spring hole for 24 hours.

00:19:26.488 --> 00:19:28.341
It's very obvious to see

00:19:28.341 --> 00:19:30.280
if the ground is hot or not.

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:32.613
I never use a thermometer.

00:19:32.613 --> 00:19:34.293
If I need to check the heat

00:19:34.293 --> 00:19:35.960
I just use this one.

00:19:37.204 --> 00:19:39.701
Our biggest challenge is rain.

00:19:39.701 --> 00:19:41.843
If it rains a lot these holes

00:19:41.843 --> 00:19:44.397
that we are using can cool down.

00:19:44.397 --> 00:19:46.227
And if they are not hot enough,

00:19:46.227 --> 00:19:49.977
obviously the bread does
not bake completely.

00:19:51.392 --> 00:19:54.443
(spry music)

00:19:54.443 --> 00:19:56.161
Everybody eats our bread,

00:19:56.161 --> 00:19:58.101
our visitors, the locals.

00:19:58.101 --> 00:20:00.836
Hot spring rye bread has a unique taste

00:20:00.836 --> 00:20:03.097
that you don't get from ovens.

00:20:03.097 --> 00:20:06.176
The texture of this
bread is quite special.

00:20:06.176 --> 00:20:09.266
It's quite heavy, it's
not a typical light bread.

00:20:09.266 --> 00:20:12.407
When you show this to
travelers that come to Iceland

00:20:12.407 --> 00:20:13.887
and you see their faces,

00:20:13.887 --> 00:20:16.465
they go "wow", then you start to think

00:20:16.465 --> 00:20:19.465
that this is quite amazing actually.

00:20:20.989 --> 00:20:23.822
(energetic music)

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:26.996
- There's lots of amazing
weather phenomenon

00:20:26.996 --> 00:20:28.828
happening all over our planet,

00:20:28.828 --> 00:20:33.418
but what's going on
outside of our atmosphere?

00:20:33.418 --> 00:20:34.653
My name is Leland Melvin,

00:20:34.653 --> 00:20:37.215
and as an astronaut I can tell you

00:20:37.215 --> 00:20:38.674
weather conditions in space

00:20:38.674 --> 00:20:41.525
can be a matter of life and death.

00:20:41.525 --> 00:20:44.108
Especially during a space walk.

00:20:46.325 --> 00:20:48.106
(elegant music)

00:20:48.106 --> 00:20:49.935
When we prepare for a space walk,

00:20:49.935 --> 00:20:51.885
we have to be able to deal

00:20:51.885 --> 00:20:53.773
with temperatures that range from

00:20:53.773 --> 00:20:57.285
positive 250 degrees Fahrenheit in the sun

00:20:57.285 --> 00:21:00.925
and minus 250 degrees in the shade.

00:21:00.925 --> 00:21:02.759
(elegant beat music)

00:21:02.759 --> 00:21:04.386
But in our space suit we have

00:21:04.386 --> 00:21:07.655
the ability to withstand
those temperature extremes

00:21:07.655 --> 00:21:09.855
because we have visors
that reflect the sun

00:21:09.855 --> 00:21:12.379
away from our eyes and
we have heated gloves

00:21:12.379 --> 00:21:14.426
as well as a heated suit.

00:21:14.426 --> 00:21:16.117
And then we have cooling

00:21:16.117 --> 00:21:17.978
that allows us to stay cool

00:21:17.978 --> 00:21:20.811
during those temperature extremes.

00:21:22.247 --> 00:21:24.517
A solar flare is a sudden
flash of brightness

00:21:24.517 --> 00:21:26.176
observed near the suns surface

00:21:26.176 --> 00:21:29.449
that emits X-ray and UV radiation.

00:21:29.449 --> 00:21:33.661
In space, the radiation
levels can be deadly.

00:21:33.661 --> 00:21:36.439
When we're notified of a solar
flare on the space station,

00:21:36.439 --> 00:21:39.030
we retreat to what we call the dog house.

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:41.999
This dog house is lined with water bags

00:21:41.999 --> 00:21:44.582
that allow us to block the radiation

00:21:44.582 --> 00:21:46.083
coming from the sun

00:21:46.083 --> 00:21:47.792
before it gets to our bodies.

00:21:47.792 --> 00:21:48.731
And that's where we go to

00:21:48.731 --> 00:21:53.539
keep from getting exposed
to high energy radiation.

00:21:53.539 --> 00:21:55.260
(mysterious music)

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:57.571
As we go further out
into our solar system,

00:21:57.571 --> 00:22:01.238
weather events become
more and more extreme.

00:22:03.451 --> 00:22:06.262
Jupiter, our solar system’s biggest planet,

00:22:06.262 --> 00:22:09.150
also features its most impressive storm,

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:11.150
the Great Red Spot.

00:22:12.619 --> 00:22:16.107
The gigantic tempest is
twice as wide as planet Earth

00:22:16.107 --> 00:22:20.267
and has winds that reach
up to 400 miles per hour.

00:22:20.267 --> 00:22:24.434
It's been swirling over
Jupiter for at least 150 years.

00:22:26.997 --> 00:22:30.726
Further out, Neptune features
its own great big storm.

00:22:30.726 --> 00:22:34.226
It takes up the planets entire atmosphere.

00:22:35.307 --> 00:22:38.238
Neptune is our solar
systems windiest planet,

00:22:38.238 --> 00:22:39.845
with wind speeds that can reach

00:22:39.845 --> 00:22:42.747
up to 1500 miles per hour.

00:22:42.747 --> 00:22:44.558
That's nine times faster

00:22:44.558 --> 00:22:48.558
than the strongest winds
ever recorded on earth.

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:52.588
To find the strongest known
winds in our universe,

00:22:52.588 --> 00:22:56.598
we have to go well
beyond our solar system.

00:22:56.598 --> 00:22:58.699
Astrophysicists at York University

00:22:58.699 --> 00:23:01.578
have discovered the
fastest winds ever recorded

00:23:01.578 --> 00:23:04.139
near a supermassive black hole.

00:23:04.139 --> 00:23:05.809
These wind speeds clock in at

00:23:05.809 --> 00:23:09.059
125 million miles per hour.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.038
I hope my crew never gets caught up

00:23:12.038 --> 00:23:13.871
in anything like that.

00:23:16.668 --> 00:23:19.335
(intense music)

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:26.531
- [Narrator] Known as
the land of fire and ice,

00:23:26.531 --> 00:23:29.040
Iceland is an island of extremes

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:32.533
where people wage a relentless
war against the elements.

00:23:32.533 --> 00:23:35.455
- It's seriously unpredictable weather.

00:23:35.455 --> 00:23:37.385
It's basically like a battle zone

00:23:37.385 --> 00:23:41.324
of different high pressure/
low pressure areas.

00:23:41.324 --> 00:23:42.991
Anything can happen.

00:23:44.305 --> 00:23:45.255
- [Narrator] Dangerous conditions

00:23:45.255 --> 00:23:48.108
mean people can find
themselves in serious trouble

00:23:48.108 --> 00:23:50.617
and in need of rescue.

00:23:50.617 --> 00:23:51.876
But there's a problem,

00:23:51.876 --> 00:23:53.988
the small country has no army

00:23:53.988 --> 00:23:56.297
or government program to help.

00:23:56.297 --> 00:23:59.364
So citizens formed the Icelandic
Search and Rescue Team,

00:23:59.364 --> 00:24:03.114
known as ICE-SAR, a
national emergency force.

00:24:04.327 --> 00:24:06.024
They're everyday people,

00:24:06.024 --> 00:24:08.847
willing to risk their lives to save others

00:24:08.847 --> 00:24:10.634
in the worst conditions imaginable.

00:24:10.634 --> 00:24:11.697
(wind blowing)

00:24:11.697 --> 00:24:13.729
- You're going out in the conditions

00:24:13.729 --> 00:24:16.677
when everyone else is
supposed to be inside.

00:24:16.677 --> 00:24:18.499
So you have to be a little bit crazy

00:24:18.499 --> 00:24:19.999
but in a good way.

00:24:24.127 --> 00:24:26.358
(adventurous music)

00:24:26.358 --> 00:24:28.649
- ICE-SAR is the community of volunteers

00:24:28.649 --> 00:24:31.267
that responds to calls for help

00:24:31.267 --> 00:24:33.889
all year around in Iceland.

00:24:33.889 --> 00:24:35.379
I think to take part in ICE-SAR

00:24:35.379 --> 00:24:37.638
you just have to be willing to put forward

00:24:37.638 --> 00:24:39.587
the skills that you have.

00:24:39.587 --> 00:24:42.969
I sometimes consider a
rescue team like a village,

00:24:42.969 --> 00:24:47.157
you need every kind of person
to make everything work.

00:24:47.157 --> 00:24:48.957
- I think it's kind of amazing

00:24:48.957 --> 00:24:52.067
because it's all volunteerss.

00:24:52.067 --> 00:24:55.485
We have professors, taxi
drivers, carpenters,

00:24:55.485 --> 00:24:59.538
plumbers, office workers, just name it.

00:24:59.538 --> 00:25:00.816
Men and women.

00:25:00.816 --> 00:25:02.885
All kinds of people.

00:25:02.885 --> 00:25:04.874
It's very hard to imagine Iceland

00:25:04.874 --> 00:25:08.041
without our association and our teams.

00:25:16.820 --> 00:25:19.213
(excited music)

00:25:19.213 --> 00:25:21.102
- [Narrator] Iceland's
breathtaking landscape

00:25:21.102 --> 00:25:23.233
attracts tourists and adventure seekers

00:25:23.233 --> 00:25:25.702
from all over the world.

00:25:25.702 --> 00:25:27.164
But that beauty can lure people

00:25:27.164 --> 00:25:29.684
to a false sense of security.

00:25:29.684 --> 00:25:31.564
- Weather conditions can change

00:25:31.564 --> 00:25:34.212
faster than anything else.

00:25:34.212 --> 00:25:35.578
You can have a sunny day,

00:25:35.578 --> 00:25:39.020
and in two minutes it's a storm.

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:42.437
Wind, rain, even snow in the summer time.

00:25:43.903 --> 00:25:46.081
- People are just going for a hike

00:25:46.081 --> 00:25:47.549
and they're pretty confident

00:25:47.549 --> 00:25:49.121
that the mountain that they're going on

00:25:49.121 --> 00:25:50.538
is not dangerous.

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:53.858
They have the lower glaciers

00:25:53.858 --> 00:25:56.561
which have become quite a
popular tourist attraction.

00:25:56.561 --> 00:25:58.500
And people are going for walks on these

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:00.102
and they are full of crevasses,

00:26:00.102 --> 00:26:02.712
extremely dangerous, super slippery.

00:26:02.712 --> 00:26:05.942
And then they fall over
and hurt themselves.

00:26:05.942 --> 00:26:07.913
Then they have to rely
on Search and Rescue

00:26:07.913 --> 00:26:09.746
to come and find them.

00:26:11.568 --> 00:26:12.926
- [Narrator] For average citizens

00:26:12.926 --> 00:26:15.401
to gain the skills needed
to be part of ICE-SAR,

00:26:15.401 --> 00:26:18.889
they go through a rigorous
18 month training program.

00:26:18.889 --> 00:26:22.586
They learn mountaineering,
climbing, and orienteering.

00:26:22.586 --> 00:26:24.778
- It takes a huge skillset,

00:26:24.778 --> 00:26:29.165
but when you can deal with any weather,

00:26:29.165 --> 00:26:32.165
then you can go out and help people.

00:26:33.155 --> 00:26:36.304
(sombre music)

00:26:36.304 --> 00:26:38.406
- Across the country I think our teams

00:26:38.406 --> 00:26:41.823
get 4-5 callers per day.

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:46.291
And our people, they're
running from their own work

00:26:46.291 --> 00:26:49.041
or their family to join the team.

00:26:51.333 --> 00:26:54.609
- When I get called out,
we have three codes.

00:26:54.609 --> 00:26:58.651
It's F1 - high priority, life at stake.

00:26:58.651 --> 00:27:01.284
F2 - not as high priority.

00:27:01.284 --> 00:27:02.867
And F3 - easygoing.

00:27:04.614 --> 00:27:06.795
When you get called out, high priority,

00:27:06.795 --> 00:27:07.934
and you get your text

00:27:07.934 --> 00:27:09.475
and it says a child,

00:27:09.475 --> 00:27:11.185
then your heart goes pumping

00:27:11.185 --> 00:27:13.606
almost out of your chest and you run out.

00:27:13.606 --> 00:27:16.892
I don't even say goodbye
to my children or wife.

00:27:16.892 --> 00:27:18.578
(heroic music)

00:27:18.578 --> 00:27:20.658
I can remember one call, a boy,

00:27:20.658 --> 00:27:22.269
got lost in Selfoss.

00:27:22.269 --> 00:27:24.887
All I could think about was my son.

00:27:24.887 --> 00:27:28.567
If he got lost I would like
to have everyone helping.

00:27:28.567 --> 00:27:32.426
So a lot of emotions comes
up when you get called up.

00:27:32.426 --> 00:27:34.105
(radio chatter)

00:27:34.105 --> 00:27:36.236
(hopeful music)

00:27:36.236 --> 00:27:38.905
- I'm proud of the unity

00:27:38.905 --> 00:27:41.269
and the fact that people
are willing to sacrifice

00:27:41.269 --> 00:27:44.186
their spare time to help others.

00:27:46.901 --> 00:27:50.033
- The feeling that I get
when we rescue someone.

00:27:50.033 --> 00:27:52.428
It doesn't have to be me
that finds the person,

00:27:52.428 --> 00:27:55.908
can be another group
searching somewhere else

00:27:55.908 --> 00:27:57.537
but we're searching for the same person.

00:27:57.537 --> 00:28:01.507
When someone gets found, you
get this wellness feeling,

00:28:01.507 --> 00:28:04.067
we did something right.

00:28:04.067 --> 00:28:06.377
- They are our heroes in so many ways.

00:28:06.377 --> 00:28:10.544
But in Icelandic culture we
don't do a lot of hero talk.

00:28:11.689 --> 00:28:12.838
- No, I'm not a hero.

00:28:12.838 --> 00:28:15.005
I'm just a part of a team.

00:28:16.409 --> 00:28:18.297
- I'm not a hero, no.

00:28:18.297 --> 00:28:20.380
I'm just a normal person.

00:28:21.377 --> 00:28:23.283
- [Jon Svanberg] I'm very
proud of the association

00:28:23.283 --> 00:28:26.181
and what they have accomplished.

00:28:26.181 --> 00:28:28.598
They've saved a lot of lives.

00:28:34.467 --> 00:28:37.134
(elegant music)

00:28:41.778 --> 00:28:43.759
- [Narrator] California's iconic sequoia's

00:28:43.759 --> 00:28:45.981
are the largest trees on earth.

00:28:45.981 --> 00:28:49.419
These giants have lived
for thousands of years

00:28:49.419 --> 00:28:54.209
surviving war, famine and
the rise of globalization.

00:28:54.209 --> 00:28:58.376
But California's ongoing
drought could mean disaster.

00:28:59.298 --> 00:29:00.801
These enormous trees are already

00:29:00.801 --> 00:29:04.468
showing signs of weakness
never seen before.

00:29:05.438 --> 00:29:07.729
Anthony Ambrose, and
a team of researchers,

00:29:07.729 --> 00:29:09.859
are heading into the forest

00:29:09.859 --> 00:29:12.001
to climb hundreds of feet in the air

00:29:12.001 --> 00:29:15.328
and see what can be done to
save these magnificent trees.

00:29:15.328 --> 00:29:17.911
(heroic music)

00:29:27.717 --> 00:29:31.267
(lighthearted music)

00:29:31.267 --> 00:29:32.408
- Sequoia National Park

00:29:32.408 --> 00:29:34.755
is just absolutely amazing.

00:29:34.755 --> 00:29:39.117
A giant sequoia is the
largest tree on the planet.

00:29:39.117 --> 00:29:41.146
And they have these huge orange trunks

00:29:41.146 --> 00:29:43.725
and massive sprawling crowns

00:29:43.725 --> 00:29:45.756
that have huge limbs.

00:29:45.756 --> 00:29:49.057
Some of the branches are
five, six feet thick.

00:29:49.057 --> 00:29:52.687
They live to be more than
three thousand years old.

00:29:52.687 --> 00:29:54.769
I'm fascinated with how they grow

00:29:54.769 --> 00:29:58.289
and how they are able to
get to be so old and so big.

00:29:58.289 --> 00:29:59.729
It's just an amazing experience

00:29:59.729 --> 00:30:04.380
to be able to study them so
intimately and so closely.

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:07.951
California's currently in the
middle of a severe drought.

00:30:07.951 --> 00:30:10.081
The giant sequoia trees
appear to be showing

00:30:10.081 --> 00:30:13.645
some symptoms of some drought stress.

00:30:13.645 --> 00:30:16.638
More stress than we've ever
measured in giant sequoia trees

00:30:16.638 --> 00:30:18.638
and we want to know why.

00:30:21.039 --> 00:30:23.071
(adventurous music)

00:30:23.071 --> 00:30:27.340
Our research entails climbing
the giant sequoia trees

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:30.780
and measuring their water status basically

00:30:30.780 --> 00:30:32.940
an index of how stressed they are

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:35.273
relative to the environment.

00:30:36.197 --> 00:30:38.740
The water status of all plants

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:41.609
is kind of like the equivalent
of blood pressure in a human.

00:30:41.609 --> 00:30:43.766
It's just a basic measure of

00:30:43.766 --> 00:30:47.512
how much water they have available to them

00:30:47.512 --> 00:30:50.262
to continue to function and grow.

00:30:56.466 --> 00:30:58.786
So we clip little shoots of foliage

00:30:58.786 --> 00:31:00.637
and put 'em in this device

00:31:00.637 --> 00:31:03.527
that is able to measure how much tension

00:31:03.527 --> 00:31:05.931
the water column is at at the time

00:31:05.931 --> 00:31:09.169
that you cut that little piece of foliage.

00:31:09.169 --> 00:31:11.457
We put the little piece of foliage,

00:31:11.457 --> 00:31:14.807
leaf end down, into this
pressurized chamber.

00:31:14.807 --> 00:31:16.257
And the amount of pressure it takes

00:31:16.257 --> 00:31:18.327
to push the water back out of the stem

00:31:18.327 --> 00:31:21.124
is equal to the amount
of tension it was under

00:31:21.124 --> 00:31:24.459
when you cut that piece of foliage.

00:31:24.459 --> 00:31:26.880
When water availability declines,

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:28.827
such as during a drought,

00:31:28.827 --> 00:31:32.494
that creates a lot more
tension in the tree.

00:31:33.734 --> 00:31:35.392
When the tension becomes too great

00:31:35.392 --> 00:31:39.122
then tiny little air
bubbles form in the wood.

00:31:39.122 --> 00:31:40.632
And when it gets bad enough

00:31:40.632 --> 00:31:44.049
then the plant suffers and starts to die.

00:31:46.405 --> 00:31:49.238
(concerned music)

00:31:51.051 --> 00:31:51.910
- There's a couple reasons

00:31:51.910 --> 00:31:54.936
why we need to climb to
the tops of these trees.

00:31:54.936 --> 00:31:58.235
(hopeful music)

00:31:58.235 --> 00:31:59.917
As water moves up the tree,

00:31:59.917 --> 00:32:02.005
it's fighting gravity and friction.

00:32:02.005 --> 00:32:03.715
And the top of the tree is gonna be

00:32:03.715 --> 00:32:05.778
the most stressed part of the tree.

00:32:05.778 --> 00:32:07.238
So in order to get a really good idea

00:32:07.238 --> 00:32:09.208
of how stressed they are,

00:32:09.208 --> 00:32:12.208
we need to go to the very tippy top.

00:32:16.092 --> 00:32:17.730
It's a really humbling experience

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:19.300
because you're up in the crown

00:32:19.300 --> 00:32:21.202
of these massive ancient things.

00:32:21.202 --> 00:32:23.619
And they make you feel small.

00:32:26.111 --> 00:32:29.261
I'm at the top of a very
large giant sequoia tree.

00:32:29.261 --> 00:32:30.370
We're waiting for the sunrise

00:32:30.370 --> 00:32:34.731
because it's really,
really pretty this morning.

00:32:34.731 --> 00:32:36.314
In order to get a really
good understanding

00:32:36.314 --> 00:32:38.165
of how the trees are
responding to the drought

00:32:38.165 --> 00:32:41.786
we need to understand
different time periods.

00:32:41.786 --> 00:32:44.034
We climb up into the giant sequoia trees

00:32:44.034 --> 00:32:46.525
at predawn hours before the sun rises

00:32:46.525 --> 00:32:48.164
and measure their water status

00:32:48.164 --> 00:32:49.925
kind of at their most relaxed state

00:32:49.925 --> 00:32:52.986
when they're not losing
water to the atmosphere.

00:32:52.986 --> 00:32:55.717
And then we also go back
in the middle of the day

00:32:55.717 --> 00:32:58.447
when they're the most
active and most stressed

00:32:58.447 --> 00:32:59.994
and measure their water status

00:32:59.994 --> 00:33:04.425
and collect leaf samples
at that point as well.

00:33:04.425 --> 00:33:07.092
(hopeful music)

00:33:13.131 --> 00:33:14.927
Giant sequoias really need fire,

00:33:14.927 --> 00:33:17.527
it's a natural part of the ecosystem here.

00:33:17.527 --> 00:33:18.657
They can use this information

00:33:18.657 --> 00:33:21.146
to prioritize where they
do prescribed burning

00:33:21.146 --> 00:33:23.269
to be able to thin out some
of the understory trees

00:33:23.269 --> 00:33:24.586
that are competing for the water

00:33:24.586 --> 00:33:25.906
and hopefully provide more water

00:33:25.906 --> 00:33:28.156
to the giant sequoia trees.

00:33:31.998 --> 00:33:33.863
It's important to realize and remember

00:33:33.863 --> 00:33:36.085
that these trees have been
living for thousands of years

00:33:36.085 --> 00:33:38.834
and they've dealt with
pretty severe events,

00:33:38.834 --> 00:33:40.885
fires and droughts, in the past.

00:33:40.885 --> 00:33:42.845
And they're amazingly resilient.

00:33:42.845 --> 00:33:43.712
One of the things that really

00:33:43.712 --> 00:33:45.112
is remarkable about these trees

00:33:45.112 --> 00:33:49.221
is their ability to recover
from drought type events.

00:33:49.221 --> 00:33:51.293
The question is, as conditions change,

00:33:51.293 --> 00:33:54.362
as temperatures continue
to increase globally,

00:33:54.362 --> 00:33:56.628
are conditions gonna change to a point

00:33:56.628 --> 00:33:58.478
where they may reach some threshold

00:33:58.478 --> 00:33:59.958
where they're not able to recover

00:33:59.958 --> 00:34:02.125
from these type of events?

00:34:04.592 --> 00:34:07.776
It's an absolutely
amazing, unique experience

00:34:07.776 --> 00:34:10.137
to be able to climb into these trees.

00:34:10.137 --> 00:34:12.937
We wanna preserve them;
we want them to be here;

00:34:12.937 --> 00:34:16.354
and we want them to be healthy and happy.

00:34:18.443 --> 00:34:21.443
(adventurous music)

00:34:22.753 --> 00:34:24.860
- [Narrator] It's a
surprisingly dangerous mission,

00:34:24.860 --> 00:34:26.484
safely navigate the skies of one

00:34:26.484 --> 00:34:30.317
of the iconic tourist
destinations in America.

00:34:31.225 --> 00:34:33.676
The helicopter pilots of Niagara Falls

00:34:33.676 --> 00:34:37.174
battle extreme wind and
unpredictable weather conditions

00:34:37.174 --> 00:34:40.886
all to give tourists that
one spectacular view.

00:34:40.886 --> 00:34:42.406
- Sometimes it is very scary,

00:34:42.406 --> 00:34:45.236
you not to let the fear overcome you

00:34:45.236 --> 00:34:46.916
so you control the aircraft,

00:34:46.916 --> 00:34:49.116
you get it back to a safe environment

00:34:49.116 --> 00:34:50.624
so you can go home at night.

00:34:50.624 --> 00:34:53.615
That's the name of the
game, go home. (chuckle)

00:34:53.615 --> 00:34:57.365
(flowing and crashing water)

00:35:06.489 --> 00:35:09.192
(hopeful music)

00:35:09.192 --> 00:35:10.382
My name's Stanley Snopkowski.

00:35:10.382 --> 00:35:12.040
I've been flying over Niagara falls

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:15.550
about 20 years, and each day is different.

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:17.520
I spent 20 years in the military.

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:20.872
I've flown in Germany, England, Vietnam,

00:35:20.872 --> 00:35:25.784
and the weather here is
nothing like anything else.

00:35:25.784 --> 00:35:28.451
(anxious music)

00:35:30.275 --> 00:35:34.025
(helicopter blades whirling)

00:35:41.409 --> 00:35:42.952
Niagara Falls is a beautiful,

00:35:42.952 --> 00:35:46.413
but like anything beautiful
there's a ugly side to it.

00:35:46.413 --> 00:35:48.523
The weather here can change quite rapidly.

00:35:48.523 --> 00:35:51.474
You can have snow showers
and five minutes later

00:35:51.474 --> 00:35:53.573
you can have bright sunny blue skies.

00:35:53.573 --> 00:35:55.165
You have to have a healthy respect for it

00:35:55.165 --> 00:35:57.859
because it can go wrong
in a matter of minutes.

00:35:57.859 --> 00:36:01.773
The winds can gust up to
30 to 40 miles an hour.

00:36:01.773 --> 00:36:03.693
When the wind comes around the buildings

00:36:03.693 --> 00:36:06.293
it tends to create mechanical turbulence,

00:36:06.293 --> 00:36:09.555
either an updraft or
downdraft or swirling.

00:36:09.555 --> 00:36:11.981
And then sometimes when you
make the turn into the wind

00:36:11.981 --> 00:36:14.395
it's almost sometimes
like hitting a brick wall.

00:36:14.395 --> 00:36:16.045
I've had instances where

00:36:16.045 --> 00:36:18.003
the aircraft wanted to come apart,

00:36:18.003 --> 00:36:19.952
quit flying and we
nursed it to the ground,

00:36:19.952 --> 00:36:21.333
we got down and we walked away.

00:36:21.333 --> 00:36:23.315
And it could have ended up differently

00:36:23.315 --> 00:36:25.715
without the training,
without the knowledge.

00:36:25.715 --> 00:36:27.256
(chuckling)

00:36:27.256 --> 00:36:29.839
(sombre music)

00:36:34.085 --> 00:36:36.609
As the only pilot onboard the aircraft,

00:36:36.609 --> 00:36:38.839
if something happens
it's my responsibility

00:36:38.839 --> 00:36:41.477
to get everybody down safely.

00:36:41.477 --> 00:36:44.178
I don't like being
scared in the helicopter.

00:36:44.178 --> 00:36:45.671
I'm not a thrill seeker,

00:36:45.671 --> 00:36:48.111
I don't take a lot of unnecessary chances.

00:36:48.111 --> 00:36:50.301
I have a skill, I have knowledge,

00:36:50.301 --> 00:36:54.171
I know my aircraft and
I know my capabilities

00:36:54.171 --> 00:36:56.493
and I try not to exceed those.

00:36:56.493 --> 00:36:57.961
I sometimes think that Mother Nature

00:36:57.961 --> 00:36:59.514
is not my friend today

00:36:59.514 --> 00:37:02.239
because of the winds or
because of the rain or snow.

00:37:02.239 --> 00:37:03.322
You deal with that.

00:37:03.322 --> 00:37:04.522
You hopefully come out on the good end.

00:37:04.522 --> 00:37:05.355
Or the other thing is

00:37:05.355 --> 00:37:07.773
is you just sit there and
say, “Okay, you win today.

00:37:07.773 --> 00:37:09.384
I'm not gonna fly."

00:37:09.384 --> 00:37:11.717
(chuckling)

00:37:16.933 --> 00:37:20.160
(adventurous music)
I look at the Falls and I see

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:21.901
the beauty of nature.

00:37:21.901 --> 00:37:25.605
Some days just after a
major storm comes through

00:37:25.605 --> 00:37:27.272
the air is so clear.

00:37:28.237 --> 00:37:31.696
You have maybe a hundred miles visibility.

00:37:31.696 --> 00:37:34.696
And it's just awe inspiring
to be able to see all that

00:37:34.696 --> 00:37:39.314
and see the falls from a
different angle, I just love it.

00:37:39.314 --> 00:37:42.231
(enchanting music)

00:37:45.765 --> 00:37:48.432
(elegant music)

00:37:50.624 --> 00:37:51.457
- [Narrator] There's a lot to love

00:37:51.457 --> 00:37:53.995
about living in a big, bright city.

00:37:53.995 --> 00:37:57.412
People, culture, late night food.

00:37:58.324 --> 00:38:00.482
But there's something missing.

00:38:00.482 --> 00:38:03.813
It's there but you can't see it.

00:38:03.813 --> 00:38:04.980
The night sky.

00:38:09.413 --> 00:38:10.329
(homely music)
This is the story

00:38:10.329 --> 00:38:12.182
about a small town in Colorado.

00:38:12.182 --> 00:38:14.674
- Actually, two small towns in Colorado.

00:38:14.674 --> 00:38:17.209
- [Narrator] That are devoted to space.

00:38:17.209 --> 00:38:20.919
- Westcliffe has a
population of 564 residents,

00:38:20.919 --> 00:38:24.336
and Silver Cliff has a population of 575.

00:38:25.501 --> 00:38:27.661
This used to be a mining community

00:38:27.661 --> 00:38:31.316
and then it evolved into
a railroad community

00:38:31.316 --> 00:38:34.354
and then into a ranching community.

00:38:34.354 --> 00:38:36.215
We're always looking to do something

00:38:36.215 --> 00:38:38.474
that will bring people into our community

00:38:38.474 --> 00:38:40.074
and lately that seems to be

00:38:40.074 --> 00:38:42.895
we need to invest in our sky.

00:38:42.895 --> 00:38:45.266
- [Narrator] So they made
light pollution their enemy.

00:38:45.266 --> 00:38:48.153
They darkened their towns
so that the night sky

00:38:48.153 --> 00:38:50.215
could become their star attraction.

00:38:50.215 --> 00:38:51.914
- Have you ever seen the Milky Way?

00:38:51.914 --> 00:38:54.913
That goes from horizon to horizon.

00:38:54.913 --> 00:38:56.803
88% of the world's population

00:38:56.803 --> 00:38:59.852
has never seen the Milky Way.

00:38:59.852 --> 00:39:02.573
It's the most magnificent
thing in the world,

00:39:02.573 --> 00:39:04.205
that's what we see here.

00:39:04.205 --> 00:39:06.344
- [Narrator] But for the towns to go dark,

00:39:06.344 --> 00:39:08.795
they needed to cover all of their lights.

00:39:08.795 --> 00:39:10.356
Not just street lamps,

00:39:10.356 --> 00:39:13.725
but the lights outside every
house and ranch near town.

00:39:13.725 --> 00:39:15.454
- That wasn't easy.

00:39:15.454 --> 00:39:18.412
This is a frontier town
and I can tell you,

00:39:18.412 --> 00:39:23.134
frontier people don't like
being told what to do.

00:39:23.134 --> 00:39:25.996
We had to win their hearts and minds.

00:39:25.996 --> 00:39:27.554
- [Narrator] It took 10 years.

00:39:27.554 --> 00:39:29.105
They passed ordinances,

00:39:29.105 --> 00:39:31.444
raised money to cover the street lights,

00:39:31.444 --> 00:39:34.685
and even built an observatory.

00:39:34.685 --> 00:39:36.590
The darker the town became,

00:39:36.590 --> 00:39:39.462
the more people saw the light.

00:39:39.462 --> 00:39:41.841
In 2016, the valley's dark sky

00:39:41.841 --> 00:39:44.932
was designated as one of
the darkest in the world,

00:39:44.932 --> 00:39:47.691
drawing visitors from all over.

00:39:47.691 --> 00:39:50.108
(calm music)

00:39:51.132 --> 00:39:54.193
(dialogue drowned out by music)

00:39:54.193 --> 00:39:57.634
- [Man] Okay, might need a
six pack of 'em. (chuckles)

00:39:57.634 --> 00:39:59.334
(background chatter)

00:39:59.334 --> 00:40:00.428
- Well I was really hoping

00:40:00.428 --> 00:40:02.500
we'd be able to see Saturn by now.

00:40:02.500 --> 00:40:04.579
- Yeah, me too.

00:40:04.579 --> 00:40:07.746
- That a community would
decide to dim its lights

00:40:07.746 --> 00:40:09.495
and help everyone to appreciate

00:40:09.495 --> 00:40:13.495
the beauty of the night
sky is an amazing thing.

00:40:15.879 --> 00:40:17.908
- You come to a place like this

00:40:17.908 --> 00:40:22.278
and the stars just

00:40:22.278 --> 00:40:25.994
burst and it's remarkable.

00:40:25.994 --> 00:40:28.411
(calm music)

00:40:31.905 --> 00:40:33.445
- Not a lot of people are lucky enough

00:40:33.445 --> 00:40:36.442
to see the dark sky as
our ancestors saw it,

00:40:36.442 --> 00:40:39.159
and I feel that we're a very special place

00:40:39.159 --> 00:40:40.943
to be able to look at the stars

00:40:40.943 --> 00:40:44.208
as people did thousands of years ago.

00:40:44.208 --> 00:40:46.559
- [Man] It's a humbling thing.

00:40:46.559 --> 00:40:49.828
Suddenly, you realize
you can almost touch it

00:40:49.828 --> 00:40:53.636
and you're now a part
of the bigger universe.

00:40:53.636 --> 00:40:55.477
And so it's just a wonderful experience

00:40:55.477 --> 00:40:56.816
to see the dark skies,

00:40:56.816 --> 00:41:00.233
and that's what we're trying to preserve.

